Below is an **initial implementation** of a high-throughput streaming data parser in **Rust**, optimized for minimal memory overhead and high performance. This example reads a large file line-by-line (streaming) and processes each line with minimal allocations.

### **Key Features:**
- **Zero-copy parsing** (avoids unnecessary allocations).
- **Asynchronous I/O** (for high throughput).
- **Minimal memory overhead** (processes data in chunks).
- **Configurable batching** (for trade-offs between latency and throughput).

---

### **Implementation (`stream_parser.rs`)**
```rust
use std::{
    fs::File,
    io::{BufRead, BufReader},
    path::Path,
};
use tokio::sync::mpsc;
use tokio::task;

// Define a custom error type for parsing failures
#[derive(Debug)]
pub enum ParseError {
    IoError(std::io::Error),
    ParseError(String),
}

// A simple data structure to hold parsed records
#[derive(Debug)]
pub struct Record {
    pub id: u64,
    pub value: String,
}

// The streaming parser struct
pub struct StreamParser {
    batch_size: usize,
    input_path: String,
}

impl StreamParser {
    pub fn new(input_path: &str, batch_size: usize) -> Self {
        Self {
            batch_size,
            input_path: input_path.to_string(),
        }
    }

    /// Processes the file in a streaming fashion, yielding batches of records
    pub async fn parse(&self) -> Result<Vec<Record>, ParseError> {
        let file = File::open(Path::new(&self.input_path))
            .map_err(|e| ParseError::IoError(e))?;
        let reader = BufReader::new(file);

        let (tx, mut rx) = mpsc::channel::<Vec<Record>>(10); // Buffered channel for batches

        // Spawn a task to read and parse the file
        task::spawn(async move {
            let mut batch = Vec::with_capacity(self.batch_size);
            for line in reader.lines() {
                let line = line.map_err(|e| ParseError::IoError(e))?;
                let record = self.parse_line(&line)?;
                batch.push(record);

                // If batch is full, send it downstream
                if batch.len() == self.batch_size {
                    tx.send(batch).await.expect("Failed to send batch");
                    batch = Vec::with_capacity(self.batch_size);
                }
            }

            // Send any remaining records
            if !batch.is_empty() {
                tx.send(batch).await.expect("Failed to send final batch");
            }

            Ok(())
        });

        // Collect all batches into a single Vec<Record>
        let mut records = Vec::new();
        while let Some(batch) = rx.recv().await {
            records.extend(batch);
        }

        Ok(records)
    }

    /// Parses a single line into a Record
    fn parse_line(&self, line: &str) -> Result<Record, ParseError> {
        let parts: Vec<&str> = line.split(',').collect();
        if parts.len() != 2 {
            return Err(ParseError::ParseError(format!(
                "Invalid line format: {}",
                line
            )));
        }

        let id = parts[0]
            .parse::<u64>()
            .map_err(|_| ParseError::ParseError("Invalid ID".to_string()))?;
        let value = parts[1].to_string();

        Ok(Record { id, value })
    }
}

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() -> Result<(), ParseError> {
    let parser = StreamParser::new("large_input.csv", 10_000); // Process 10k records per batch
    let records = parser.parse().await?;

    println!("Processed {} records", records.len());
    Ok(())
}
```

